  "narrative": {
    "intro": "California has received $54B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The state has 228,296 awards with an average award size of $237K. Obligations fell 13.0% year over year. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer in the state, obligating $29B. The Department of Energy obligated $6.6B and the Department of Homeland Security obligated $5.9B. LAWRENCE LIVERMORE NATIONAL SECURITY, LLC is the top vendor with $3.6B in obligations, followed by NATIONAL STEEL AND SHIPBUILDING COMPANY with $2.8B and FISHER SAND & GRAVEL CO with $2.5B. Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology) is the largest industry category at $7.2B, followed by Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences at $5.6B and Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $4.8B. Fiscal-year obligations peaked at $62B in FY25 and are currently $54B in FY26 to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated is federal buying in California?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the top buyer, obligating $29B. The next largest buyers are the Department of Energy with $6.6B and the Department of Homeland Security with $5.9B. The top ten agencies together include civilian and defense buyers such as NASA at $3.6B and the Department of Veterans Affairs at $2.2B."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which companies and industries dominate federal work in California?",
        "body": "LAWRENCE LIVERMORE NATIONAL SECURITY, LLC leads vendors with $3.6B in obligations, followed by NATIONAL STEEL AND SHIPBUILDING COMPANY at $2.8B and FISHER SAND & GRAVEL CO at $2.5B. Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology) is the largest industry at $7.2B, with related R&D at $5.6B and Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $4.8B rounding out the top sectors."
      }
    ]
